Let's walk through these numbers against what we expected — this is exactly the kind of cross-check that's worth doing deliberately, not just glancing at.

The consistency check that matters most: total_brand_replies_seen: 42253

This exactly matches the Delta total from brand_volumes.csv back when we did brand selection. That's not a coincidence — it means two independently-written pieces of code (the earlier exploration script and this pipeline) agree on how many Delta replies exist in the full dataset. That's a real, meaningful sanity check, worth noting in the decision log: "total_brand_replies_seen cross-validated against independent brand-volume analysis — exact match."

The DM-redirect rate needs an honest correction

We estimated ~45% DM-redirects earlier — but that number came from a tiny 93-row sample mixing multiple brands, several of which (comcastcares, TMobileHelp) we already identified as unusually DM-heavy. The real, full-dataset, Delta-specific number is:

6,331 / 34,435 = 18.4%

That's meaningfully lower than our early guess, but it's actually consistent with our later, more targeted look — when we manually read Delta's sample threads specifically, only 1 of 4 (25%) were DM-redirects, not the ~45% average. With 34k real pairs instead of 4 samples, 18.4% is the trustworthy number now. This is worth writing into the decision log exactly like this: "Initial ~45% DM-redirect estimate came from a small mixed-brand sample; full-dataset Delta-specific rate is 18.4%. This is part of why we chose Delta — lower DM-redirect rate than telecom brands, confirmed at scale." That's a good, honest self-correction — exactly the kind of thing the "misleading headline number" section rewards you for catching.

thread_nonroot_count: 10,274 (30% of kept pairs) confirms a real finding

Last time, we noticed by eye that some customer messages in the sample were mid-thread fragments ("@Delta 276") rather than genuine opening complaints. Now we have the real scale of it: 30% of all pairs have a customer message that isn't a fresh complaint. That's not a rare edge case — it's a third of the data. Good thing we built is_thread_root tagging into Phase 1 rather than discovering this later while labelling the golden set.

Retention math checks out

42,253 - (56 + 48 + 35 + 2,165 + 5,514) = 34,435 ✓ — matches kept_pairs exactly, so nothing is silently disappearing or double-counted. 81.5% retention overall, losing most of that to duplicates (13%) and too-short (5%) — both expected and reasonable, nothing alarming.